<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margiana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margiana was a historical region centred on the oasis of the Murghab river in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>, around the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>. Home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> sites such as Gonur Tepe, it later formed a province of the Achaemenid, Seleucid and Parthian empires and was a key stage on the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margiana.jpg" alt="Margiana" width="250" height="115" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: self, based on WP locator maps Category:Locator maps · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margiana-300BCE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Margiana, called Marguš in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> and Marv in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>, was a historical region centred on the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>. It lay in the valley of the Murghab River, which rises in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, passes through Murghab District in modern Afghanistan and reaches the oasis of Merv in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>. Margiana bordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> to the south-west, Aria in the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> in the east and Sogdia in the north. It was a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> within the Achaemenid satrapy of Bactria and a province under its successors, the Seleucid, Parthian and Sasanian empires. It is considered part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> civilisation, the Bactria-Margiana Archaeological Complex, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> civilisation, and historians disagree about its history before the Achaemenid conquest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered Margiana between 545 and 539 BCE and is said to have founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>. A revolt there led by Frâda (Phraates) after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>'s victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Gaumata</a> was suppressed in December 521 BCE by Dadarsi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> claims that 55,423 Margians were killed and 6,972 taken captive. In 328 BCE Alexander the Great sent Craterus to fortify Margiana, and Merv was re-founded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in Margiana. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-diadochi">Wars of the Diadochi</a> it came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>. Under Antiochus I Soter the oasis was surrounded by a wall over 300 km long and the city re-founded as Antiochia in Margiana, capital of a separate satrapy, to secure the routes east.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia">Mithridates I of Parthia</a> conquered Margiana about 170 BCE. Saka invaders passed through it about 140 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> held it until Mithridates II re-established control in 115 BCE. In 53 BCE, 10,000 Roman prisoners taken at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Battle of Carrhae</a> were settled at Antiochia in Margiana. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> defeated the last Parthian king in 224 CE, Margiana submitted as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> about 260. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> held it from about 480 to 565. The last Sasanian king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a>, fled to Margiana after the defeat at Nihawand, and was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> general Ahnaf ibn Qais at the Battle of Oxus River; he was killed by a Margian miller, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><p>Its place on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> gave Margiana a varied religious life. Most of the population practised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, but Buddhist, Christian, Manichaean and Jewish communities also existed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> was a major centre of Buddhist learning, a Manichaean community is known from the mid third century CE, and a Nestorian diocese of Merv is known from 424 CE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Margiana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It borrowed distinctive beliefs and practices from the Bactria–<b>Margiana</b> culture, supported by at least 383 non-Indo-European words, among them the god Indra and the ritual drink soma.</blockquote><a href="/religion/vedic-religion">Vedic religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Oxus civilisation is the common name for what archaeologists formally call the Bactria–<b>Margiana</b> Archaeological Complex (BMAC), a Middle Bronze Age civilisation of southern Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Their irrigation technology had first developed in the Bactria-<b>Margiana</b> Archaeological Complex west of the Pamirs, though how it reached the Tarim is unclear, and their staple crops, wheat and barley, also came from the west.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>They moved through the Bactria–<b>Margiana</b> area of present-day northern Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory, later called Dahistan, bordered the farming lands of Hyrcania and <b>Margiana</b>, and they traded and raided along this edge.</blockquote><a href="/state/dahae">Dahae</a></li></ul></section>
